content.thinking:
The pattern: publish, close, then stats() → expected 1, got 0. So after close, `reader` is null or stale (seq 0). Why?

The reader was primed at open (view from initial catchup — empty bus, seq 0). Then publish happened — the drain committed. But the reader was never refreshed after the drain! I decided "leave reader alone after drains; lazy catchup handles it" — lazy catchup happens in ensureReader, but after close, stats() uses `closed ? reader : ensureReader()` — the FROZEN reader, which was primed at open (empty) and never updated because no read happened between publish and close!

So the frozen reader is stale (open-time state). The old behavior: stats after close returned the writer-view scalars, which WERE updated by the drain.

Fix options:
1. After close, stats/usage fall back to reader only if it's at least as fresh as scalars... messy.
2. Update the reader during drains too — after a drain commits, the writer view is updated; also bump the reader incrementally. The drain knows exactly what it appended (it updates cursor.off). I could call `reader.catchup()`... but reader.catchup reads from disk — under the lock, cheap (only new bytes). Actually simplest: after any writer-view mutation (drain commit, replay marker, position write), if reader exists, run reader.catchup() (best-effort) to pull the new committed bytes. That's the same incremental read — cheap. Or directly apply the same units to the reader.

