input.new_string:
The merged state is kept between drains by an incremental reader of the active log tail (keyed by device/inode and the log's first-line fingerprint), so the write path never does the full directory walk; compaction and truncation (which replace the head) transparently fall back to one full scan. The drain also holds a short, bounded coalescing window while more than one live session shares the lease: requests staged while it waited for the mutex (or a heartbeat after its first gather) are re-gathered into the **same** group, so genuinely concurrent writers across processes land in one bracketed commit rather than taking a second lock round. The epoch credential is re-validated at every pipeline boundary (lock entry, decision, each flush chunk, commit, settlement): a holder taken over while queued or mid group fails its whole request, and its group either never commits or is filtered wholesale by the epoch fence — no stale byte and no stale staging file mixes into the log. A write or fsync failure rolls the entire group back physically, leaving the bus byte-for-byte as it was. A crash leaves either the complete group or none of it: the uncommitted bracket/torn tail is severed and the decision reconciles the survivors on the next open.

## Read view

Reads are decoupled from the write chain entirely. `read`, `readRange`, the data side of `replay`, position lookups, `stats` and `usage` are served from a read-only view that never takes the write mutex and never walks the directory on the hot path:

- a steady-state refresh stats the active log and parses only the bytes appended since the previous look, so fetching a range costs in proportion to the batch returned and **does not grow with log length**;
- a structural change (a segment seal, a truncation, or a compaction replacing the log head) is detected from the device/inode pair and the log's first-line fingerprint and swaps in one coherent rebuild, so a read racing a snapshot switch observes the whole old view or the whole new one — the same range fetched either side agrees message for message;
- reads always observe a **committed prefix**: a commit group is either wholly visible or wholly invisible (an in-flight bracket is skipped), sequences are continuous with no repeats, and the cumulative stats and positions only move forward;
- message payloads are located, not cached — each committed message is an offset/length locator and the record is read back from its segment or the active log, so the view's memory stays bounded by the message *counts*, not their bodies (snapshot-folded records remain parsed).

Reads therefore keep working while writers commit continuously, and a displaced (fenced) instance keeps reading as before: only its writes and truncations are invalidated. `readRange`, `stats` and `usage` also remain available after `close`, serving the last committed cache. Only two read-adjacent acts still take the mutex: the replay counter line written by `replay` and the auto-registration of an unknown consumer by `read`/`register`/`advance`.

