Wait — there's a problem with my adaptive gating: the coalescing test has A drain with only A's request in the first gather (B stages 60ms later) → othersActive = false → NO window → B lands in a second group. The test would fail!

Hmm. So the "othersActive" gate defeats the deterministic test AND the core scenario (B's request arrives after A's gather). The gate only helps when the first gather already mixed sessions.

The fundamental tension: to merge late arrivals you must WAIT, and waiting costs throughput when nobody else is writing. Options:

1. Gate on lease.sessions.length > 1 only (previous version): 10ms quiet per drain whenever ≥2 sessions are alive, even if one is idle. Throughput cost in that scenario.

2. Gate on othersActive (new version): zero cost unless requests already mixed — but misses the "B stages 1ms after A's gather" case entirely.

Hmm. What's the actual requirement? "排空要连等锁期间新到的请求一起收进提交组" — requests arriving WHILE WAITING FOR THE LOCK must be collected. When a process waits for the lock, SOMEONE ELSE holds it and is draining. The waiter stages, then waits. The current holder's drain — if it's past its gather — misses the waiter's request. The waiter then gets the lock and drains its own request (possibly merging with other waiters' requests staged during ITS wait).

Key insight: the waiter's request, staged while waiting, will be gathered by SOME drain — either the current holder's (if still gathering) or its own. The requirement "收进提交组" (collect into THE commit group) — which group? If the current holder already passed its gather, the waiter's request forms the NEXT group — still "a commit group". The baseline ALREADY collects requests staged during the lock wait (gather happens after lock acquisition). So the literal requirement is satisfied by the baseline!

The ADDITIONAL value of the merge window is only for the "staged microseconds after the gather" case. Given the throughput concern ("写入吞吐不因读并行而回退" and general), maybe the right scope is: the window should be SMALL and only run when there's evidence of concurrent activity. But "evidence" (othersActive in first gather) misses the exact case the window exists for...

Alternative: gate on LOCK CONTENTION actually observed by THIS drain's lock acquisition. withLockSync spins on EEXIST — if we had to spin (someone else held the lock recently), there's real concurrency → enable the window. If we got the lock instantly, no one else is actively draining → skip the window. This is elegant: the window runs exactly when the lock is contended (multiple writers actively draining), which is precisely when late arrivals are likely.

How to plumb: withLockSync returns fn()'s result. I can track contention: in withLockSync, record whether we had to wait (EEXIST at least once) into a variable `lastLockContended`. drainLocked reads it. But drainLocked is called via withOwnership → withLockSync — the contention flag would be set during THIS withOwnership call. Let me add a closure variable:

```js
  let lockContended = false;
  const withLockSync = (fn, waitMs = LOCK_WAIT_MS) => {
    const started = Date.now();
    let lastBeat = Date.now();
    let contended = false;
    for (;;) {
      try {
        mkdirSync(lockPath);
        ...
        break;
      } catch (err) {
        if (err.code !== 'EEXIST') throw ...;
        contended = true;
        ...
      }
      ...
    }
    lockContended = contended;
    try {
      return fn();
    } finally { ... }
  };
```

Hmm — but withLockSync is used by many callers (close, prime, etc.). Setting a flag as a side effect is a bit ugly but workable. Actually — cleaner: withLockSync could return the result AND we track contention only for the drain path. Since drainLocked is invoked inside withOwnership → withLockSync, the flag is set right before drainLocked runs. 

Gate: `mergeMs > 0 && (lockContended || othersActive)`. 

Scenario check (my deterministic test): A gets lock first — was it contended? B hasn't staged yet at t=0 → B isn't waiting → A gets lock instantly → contended=false, othersActive=false → NO window → B at t=60ms lands in group 2. TEST FAILS again.

Ugh. The deterministic test scenario (A alone at gather, B arrives later) fundamentally requires A to WAIT with no signal that B is coming. The only signals available: multiple live sessions (idle or not), lock contention (past), other-session requests (present).

OK let me reconsider the requirement once more: "排空要连等锁期间新到的请求一起收进提交组。真实并发下多个写者的追加与批量要能合进同一个提交组". 

Reading it again: "the drain must collect, into the commit group, also the requests that newly arrive during the lock wait". I think the most defensible reading: when the drain runs, it must gather requests that were staged while it was waiting for the lock — i.e., gather AFTER acquiring the lock (baseline does this), AND — the gap — ALSO requests that arrive while it HOLDS the lock waiting/working (the drain's own critical section). The BUS_DRAIN_DELAY_MS seam exists precisely to widen that window for tests. The baseline's gap: requests arriving DURING the drain (after gather) are not collected.

So the merge window IS the right fix; the question is just the gating. Given "写入吞吐不因读并行而回退" is about READS (and reads don't touch the lock at all now), the 10ms quiet window under multi-session leases is... still a write-throughput regression vs baseline in the idle-peer scenario. But is there a test for that? Baseline had NO window at all, so any window is a regression in the idle case. The task says the drain MUST collect late arrivals — that inherently requires waiting. A bounded wait is the design; the lease.sessions.length > 1 gate is the reasonable scope (single-writer buses — the common case — pay nothing).

Decision: revert to the `lease.sessions.length > 1` gate (no othersActive requirement). This makes my deterministic test pass (A waits in the window because 2 sessions exist; B arrives; merged). The idle-peer cost is ≤ mergeMs (10ms default) per drain — bounded and configurable. This matches the task's explicit requirement most directly.

Actually — hmm, can I reduce the idle cost while keeping determinism? The window exits after `mergeMs` of QUIET. In the idle case, the first re-gather (after ~2ms) finds nothing new... but the loop continues until 10ms of quiet — i.e., ~10ms. What if the window exits early when NO new requests appear in the FIRST re-gather AND no other session has staged recently? That's the same as a single 2ms probe — misses B at 60ms. For the deterministic test I need the window to genuinely wait ~mergeMs.

Compromise: keep the full quiet window (mergeMs) but make the DEFAULT smaller? The test sets BUS_DRAIN_MERGE_MS=200 explicitly, so default doesn't affect the test. Default 10ms is fine.

Revert to sessions.length > 1 gate.

